WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:11.656 --> 00:12.726 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

00:14.866 --> 00:18.576 align:middle
Earlier today the Federal Open
Market Committee announced a 1/2

00:18.626 --> 00:21.306 align:middle
percentage point reduction
in the target range

00:21.306 --> 00:23.616 align:middle
for the federal funds
rate, bringing that range

00:23.616 --> 00:26.236 align:middle
to 1 to 1-1/4 percent.

00:28.116 --> 00:29.606 align:middle
My colleagues and
I took this action

00:29.606 --> 00:33.376 align:middle
to help the U.S. economy keep
strong in the face of new risks

00:33.376 --> 00:34.426 align:middle
to the economic outlook.

00:36.046 --> 00:38.366 align:middle
The fundamentals of the
U.S. economy remain strong.

00:39.246 --> 00:41.826 align:middle
The unemployment rate has
been near half-century lows

00:41.866 --> 00:42.816 align:middle
for well more than a year,

00:43.696 --> 00:45.476 align:middle
the pace of job gains
has been solid,

00:45.716 --> 00:47.046 align:middle
and wages have been rising.

00:48.466 --> 00:51.396 align:middle
These strong labor market
conditions have underpinned

00:51.636 --> 00:54.766 align:middle
solid household spending,
which has been the key driver

00:54.766 --> 00:56.486 align:middle
of economic growth
over the past year.

00:57.906 --> 01:00.786 align:middle
At the time of our FOMC
meeting in January,

01:01.116 --> 01:03.946 align:middle
prospects for continued economic
growth remained favorable,

01:04.696 --> 01:07.216 align:middle
and we judged that monetary
policy was well positioned

01:07.356 --> 01:08.326 align:middle
to support that outlook.

01:09.726 --> 01:10.776 align:middle
Since then, the spread

01:10.776 --> 01:13.706 align:middle
of the coronavirus has brought
new challenges and risks.

01:14.756 --> 01:17.446 align:middle
The virus has afflicted many
communities around the world,

01:17.706 --> 01:20.136 align:middle
and our thoughts and prayers go
out to those who've been harmed.

01:21.806 --> 01:24.316 align:middle
The outbreak has also
disrupted economic activity

01:24.486 --> 01:27.156 align:middle
in many countries and has
prompted significant movements

01:27.156 --> 01:28.146 align:middle
in financial markets.

01:29.356 --> 01:31.326 align:middle
The virus and the measures
that are being taken

01:31.326 --> 01:36.096 align:middle
to contain it will surely weigh
on economic activity both here

01:36.096 --> 01:37.376 align:middle
and abroad for some time.

01:37.586 --> 01:40.486 align:middle
We are beginning to see
the effects on the tourism

01:40.486 --> 01:43.296 align:middle
and travel industries, and
we are hearing concerns

01:43.296 --> 01:45.826 align:middle
from industries that rely
on global supply chains.

01:46.946 --> 01:49.116 align:middle
The magnitude and persistence
of the overall effects

01:49.116 --> 01:51.796 align:middle
on the economy, however,
remain highly uncertain,

01:52.386 --> 01:54.296 align:middle
and the situation
remains a fluid one.

01:56.696 --> 01:59.596 align:middle
Against this background, the
Committee judged that the risks

01:59.646 --> 02:01.666 align:middle
to the U.S. outlook
have changed materially.

02:02.486 --> 02:05.396 align:middle
In response, we have eased
the stance of monetary policy

02:05.456 --> 02:07.456 align:middle
to provide some more
support to the economy.

02:08.756 --> 02:10.246 align:middle
Of course, the ultimate
solutions

02:10.246 --> 02:11.866 align:middle
to this challenge
will come from others,

02:12.356 --> 02:14.296 align:middle
particularly health
professionals.

02:15.326 --> 02:17.106 align:middle
We can and will do
our part, however,

02:17.136 --> 02:19.996 align:middle
to keep the U.S. economy strong
as we meet this challenge.

02:21.406 --> 02:23.096 align:middle
As always, our actions
are guided

02:23.346 --> 02:24.646 align:middle
by our congressional mandate

02:24.646 --> 02:27.316 align:middle
to promote maximum
employment and price stability.

02:29.416 --> 02:31.106 align:middle
In the weeks and months
ahead, we will continue

02:31.106 --> 02:33.666 align:middle
to closely monitor developments
and their implications

02:33.666 --> 02:37.416 align:middle
for the economic outlook, and
we will use our tools and act

02:37.476 --> 02:39.186 align:middle
as appropriate to
support the economy.

02:40.346 --> 02:43.596 align:middle
Thank you, and I'll be happy
to take a few questions.

02:43.826 --> 02:45.106 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

02:45.676 --> 02:45.956 align:middle
Thank you.

02:45.956 --> 02:51.846 align:middle
Nick Timiraos with the
Wall Street Journal.

02:52.096 --> 02:52.826 align:middle
Two questions.

02:52.886 --> 02:55.046 align:middle
First, what changed
between last week,

02:55.226 --> 02:57.336 align:middle
when many of your
colleagues seemed

02:57.366 --> 02:59.106 align:middle
to indicate it was
still too soon

02:59.136 --> 03:02.776 align:middle
to tell how this might influence
the outlook-what changed

03:02.776 --> 03:06.076 align:middle
between last week and today?

03:06.456 --> 03:08.686 align:middle
And then, how much
confidence do you have

03:08.956 --> 03:12.176 align:middle
that there will be a quick and
relatively complete recovery

03:12.176 --> 03:15.936 align:middle
of economic activity after the
peak of this virus has passed?

03:16.276 --> 03:16.866 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

03:17.486 --> 03:18.486 align:middle
So, what changed?

03:18.486 --> 03:21.756 align:middle
Of course, we've been carefully
monitoring the situation

03:22.166 --> 03:25.046 align:middle
since it first became
known and waiting

03:25.046 --> 03:26.046 align:middle
to see how it would evolve.

03:26.246 --> 03:29.396 align:middle
And I think we've
come to the view now

03:29.396 --> 03:31.266 align:middle
that this-that it is
time for us to act

03:31.266 --> 03:32.346 align:middle
in support of the economy.

03:32.346 --> 03:34.186 align:middle
And once you reach
that decision,

03:34.286 --> 03:36.456 align:middle
we decided to go ahead.

03:36.576 --> 03:39.986 align:middle
So what changed, really, was,
I would say, over the course

03:39.986 --> 03:42.636 align:middle
of the last couple weeks,
we've seen a broader spread

03:42.636 --> 03:44.526 align:middle
of the virus-we've seen it begin

03:44.526 --> 03:46.936 align:middle
to spread a bit here
in the United States.

03:47.426 --> 03:48.976 align:middle
But, for us, what really
matters, of course,

03:49.336 --> 03:53.556 align:middle
is not the epidemiology,
but the risk to the economy.

03:53.556 --> 03:55.836 align:middle
So we saw a risk to the outlook

03:55.836 --> 03:57.916 align:middle
for the economy and
chose to act.

03:57.916 --> 03:59.156 align:middle
In terms of my confidence

03:59.156 --> 04:01.376 align:middle
that we will return
to-what was your question?

04:01.666 --> 04:02.416 align:middle
Was that a question?

04:02.416 --> 04:03.086 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

04:03.086 --> 04:06.606 align:middle
How do you expect this to-the
economy to recover from this?

04:06.606 --> 04:09.576 align:middle
Do you see it as a persistent
decline in activity or something

04:09.576 --> 04:11.696 align:middle
that will be relatively
temporary and short lived?

04:11.696 --> 04:12.146 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

04:12.146 --> 04:14.226 align:middle
I don't think anybody
knows how long it will be.

04:14.226 --> 04:18.366 align:middle
I do know that the U.S. economy
is strong, and we will get

04:18.366 --> 04:19.246 align:middle
to the other side of this.

04:19.326 --> 04:24.046 align:middle
And I fully expect it will
return to solid growth

04:24.046 --> 04:25.576 align:middle
and a solid labor
market as well.

04:26.576 --> 04:28.396 align:middle
JIM TANKERSLEY.

04:29.006 --> 04:31.646 align:middle
Hi, Mr. Chairman.

04:31.646 --> 04:33.466 align:middle
Jim Tankersley, New York Times.

04:33.466 --> 04:35.516 align:middle
You said that "the ultimate
solutions" will come

04:35.516 --> 04:37.986 align:middle
from others, and I have
two questions about that.

04:37.986 --> 04:40.616 align:middle
First, is this a
coordinated-part

04:40.616 --> 04:42.776 align:middle
of a coordinated action
with other central banks,

04:42.776 --> 04:45.736 align:middle
and should we be expecting
to see more as part of that?

04:45.736 --> 04:48.166 align:middle
And, second, what
would you like to see

04:48.166 --> 04:50.766 align:middle
from American fiscal
policymakers

04:50.886 --> 04:53.226 align:middle
in response to these
new threats?

04:54.216 --> 04:54.776 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

04:54.926 --> 04:56.016 align:middle
Okay. So, first question.

04:56.236 --> 04:59.136 align:middle
We-we're in active
discussions with central banks

04:59.136 --> 05:01.636 align:middle
around the world on an ongoing
basis, as you would guess.

05:02.096 --> 05:04.666 align:middle
I've been in regular contact
with central bank leaders

05:04.666 --> 05:05.526 align:middle
from around the world.

05:05.966 --> 05:06.996 align:middle
That will continue.

05:07.766 --> 05:10.046 align:middle
Central banks are
doing what makes sense

05:10.046 --> 05:12.106 align:middle
in their particular
institutional contexts,

05:12.166 --> 05:15.026 align:middle
but we're all talking to each
other on an ongoing basis.

05:15.026 --> 05:17.286 align:middle
And our action today represents
what we think is the right

05:17.346 --> 05:21.916 align:middle
policy for us in our
particular institutional context

05:21.916 --> 05:24.406 align:middle
under our domestic mandate.

05:24.766 --> 05:28.006 align:middle
You saw this morning's G-7
statement of finance ministers

05:28.006 --> 05:29.236 align:middle
and governors, and I think

05:29.236 --> 05:32.646 align:middle
that statement does reflect
coordination at a high level

05:32.646 --> 05:35.306 align:middle
in the form of a commitment
to use all available tools,

05:35.306 --> 05:38.026 align:middle
including health-care
policy, fiscal policy,

05:38.126 --> 05:39.906 align:middle
and monetary policy
as "appropriate."

05:41.036 --> 05:45.646 align:middle
So, in terms of fiscal
policy, again, not our role.

05:45.646 --> 05:48.326 align:middle
We have-we have a full
plate with monetary policy.

05:48.326 --> 05:50.776 align:middle
Not our role to give advice
to the fiscal policymakers,

05:50.826 --> 05:53.796 align:middle
but you saw the mention
in the G-7 statement-as

05:53.926 --> 05:55.926 align:middle
"appropriate" as well.

05:57.026 --> 05:58.146 align:middle
HEATHER SCOTT.

05:58.786 --> 06:01.246 align:middle
The G-7-Heather Scott with AFP.

06:01.246 --> 06:04.626 align:middle
The G-7 statement seemed
to indicate that-I'm sorry.

06:04.726 --> 06:08.986 align:middle
I was away from the mic.

06:09.236 --> 06:10.416 align:middle
Sorry, Heather Scott with AFP.

06:10.416 --> 06:11.856 align:middle
The G-7 statement seemed

06:11.856 --> 06:16.086 align:middle
to indicate that-that
policymakers would use tools

06:16.086 --> 06:19.436 align:middle
but were not going to
do anything imminent.

06:19.436 --> 06:22.846 align:middle
Does this contradict that,
or is this in keeping

06:22.846 --> 06:26.236 align:middle
with the action-the statement
by the G-7 this morning?

06:26.696 --> 06:32.196 align:middle
And, can I ask, what would cause
you to make-take another step?

06:32.656 --> 06:34.076 align:middle
What would you be looking for,

06:34.246 --> 06:36.286 align:middle
in terms of the economic
outlook or data?

06:36.716 --> 06:37.286 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

06:37.346 --> 06:40.706 align:middle
So the sense of the
G-7, you know,

06:40.706 --> 06:43.856 align:middle
is to-we have seven
countries, obviously,

06:43.856 --> 06:47.056 align:middle
and different policies,
different situations,

06:47.096 --> 06:48.846 align:middle
different mandates-a
lot of overlap.

06:48.846 --> 06:51.636 align:middle
And the sense of that is to
get together as a group and say

06:51.636 --> 06:54.066 align:middle
at a high level that "These are
the things we're going to do.

06:54.066 --> 06:57.286 align:middle
We are going to use our
tools-all of our tools

06:57.486 --> 07:00.436 align:middle
in a strong way to-to try
to support the economy."

07:00.436 --> 07:02.556 align:middle
So that's a statement
of general support.

07:03.086 --> 07:05.396 align:middle
I think within that
you will see actions.

07:05.396 --> 07:06.366 align:middle
You've seen our action.

07:06.366 --> 07:08.056 align:middle
And I think it's

07:08.056 --> 07:10.706 align:middle
up to individual
countries-individual fiscal

07:10.706 --> 07:14.226 align:middle
policies and individual central
banks-to do what they're going

07:14.226 --> 07:15.066 align:middle
to do.

07:15.066 --> 07:18.696 align:middle
I would think it will be-it's
possible there will be some

07:19.016 --> 07:22.906 align:middle
more-more formal considered
coordination as we move forward.

07:23.636 --> 07:30.336 align:middle
In terms of-in terms of moving
forward, I would say that we do

07:30.336 --> 07:32.166 align:middle
like our current policy stance.

07:32.166 --> 07:36.006 align:middle
We think-we think it's
appropriate to-to support

07:36.006 --> 07:37.576 align:middle
achievement of our
dual-mandate goals.

07:38.226 --> 07:40.796 align:middle
But as I said in my-in my
statement, we're prepared

07:40.796 --> 07:43.146 align:middle
to use our tools-tools
and act appropriately,

07:43.666 --> 07:46.216 align:middle
depending on the flow of events.

07:46.216 --> 07:46.456 align:middle
HEATHER SCOTT.

07:46.606 --> 07:47.966 align:middle
But anything you'll be
particularly watching

07:48.166 --> 07:49.166 align:middle
that would change that?

07:49.166 --> 07:49.536 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

07:49.536 --> 07:50.676 align:middle
I can't point to any one thing.

07:50.676 --> 07:53.816 align:middle
It's always a range of things.

07:55.236 --> 07:55.706 align:middle
HEATHER LONG.

07:55.706 --> 07:57.596 align:middle
Heather Long from
the Washington Post.

07:57.596 --> 08:00.716 align:middle
There's been some rising
concern about credit markets

08:00.716 --> 08:03.976 align:middle
and possible insolvencies and
defaults either from businesses

08:03.976 --> 08:06.356 align:middle
or individuals from
the coronavirus.

08:06.356 --> 08:09.616 align:middle
Can you speak to-is the
FOMC talking about this?

08:09.616 --> 08:12.886 align:middle
Are we likely to see
any emergency provisions

08:12.946 --> 08:16.456 align:middle
from the CRA activated or things
that you normally do during,

08:16.706 --> 08:19.206 align:middle
you know, a hurricane
or that type

08:19.206 --> 08:20.596 align:middle
of disruption to the economy?

08:20.596 --> 08:20.856 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

08:20.856 --> 08:22.806 align:middle
So we don't-we don't see
any of that happening yet.

08:23.036 --> 08:25.626 align:middle
Of course, we are-we
are thinking

08:25.626 --> 08:28.026 align:middle
about what we can do
should those things happen.

08:28.026 --> 08:29.406 align:middle
There's no evidence of it yet.

08:29.406 --> 08:31.776 align:middle
The economy continues to
perform well-perform well.

08:32.106 --> 08:35.956 align:middle
As I mentioned, we do hear
concerns, particularly

08:35.956 --> 08:38.756 align:middle
from those most directly
exposed, but there's nothing

08:38.806 --> 08:41.486 align:middle
in that nature: Financial
markets are functioning

08:41.796 --> 08:43.486 align:middle
in an orderly manner and
all that sort of thing.

08:43.896 --> 08:47.916 align:middle
I think when it-when it comes to
those sorts of issues, though,

08:47.916 --> 08:50.696 align:middle
we will be-the supervisors will
be working with banks to assure

08:50.696 --> 08:53.946 align:middle
that they work with
their-with their borrowers

08:54.126 --> 08:54.816 align:middle
and that sort of thing.

08:54.816 --> 08:57.556 align:middle
So I can imagine us doing
those sorts of things,

08:57.556 --> 09:02.766 align:middle
but it's-those things are
not upon us at the moment.

09:02.856 --> 09:03.846 align:middle
BRENDAN GREELEY.

09:04.206 --> 09:04.816 align:middle
Thank you.

09:04.816 --> 09:06.556 align:middle
Brendan Greeley,
Financial Times.

09:06.726 --> 09:10.306 align:middle
Has the Committee discussed any
other monetary policy tools,

09:10.306 --> 09:12.716 align:middle
in addition to rate cuts,
and the pacing and timing

09:12.716 --> 09:14.006 align:middle
of when they might
be appropriate?

09:14.536 --> 09:14.936 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

09:14.936 --> 09:15.926 align:middle
Well, as you know,
we're in the middle

09:15.926 --> 09:17.556 align:middle
of a review of all of our tools.

09:17.596 --> 09:19.556 align:middle
So if you go back a few
meetings, we've talked

09:19.556 --> 09:22.236 align:middle
about what our toolkit is, and
we've put that in the minutes.

09:22.576 --> 09:23.926 align:middle
But in the current context, no.

09:24.016 --> 09:26.966 align:middle
What we discussed is the
current stance of policy,

09:26.966 --> 09:29.926 align:middle
is it appropriate-came to the
view that it was appropriate

09:29.926 --> 09:36.026 align:middle
to make a change and went
ahead and did that today.

09:36.546 --> 09:36.826 align:middle
RICH MILLER.

09:36.876 --> 09:38.106 align:middle
Hi. Rich Miller with Bloomberg.

09:38.106 --> 09:40.466 align:middle
Thank you very much for
having a press conference.

09:40.726 --> 09:43.506 align:middle
Can you take us a little-through
the reasoning behind the rate

09:43.506 --> 09:44.866 align:middle
cut a little bit more in depth?

09:44.976 --> 09:46.986 align:middle
You know, was there a
discuss-some economists say this

09:46.986 --> 09:50.286 align:middle
is a supply-side
shock, you know,

09:50.486 --> 09:52.876 align:middle
rate cuts are not
particularly suitable for this.

09:52.876 --> 09:55.556 align:middle
Others say, well, the
supply shock may morph

09:55.556 --> 09:56.486 align:middle
into a demand shock.

09:56.486 --> 09:59.446 align:middle
I wonder if you could just
give us a little bit more depth

09:59.526 --> 10:01.726 align:middle
on the reasoning
behind the rate cut.

10:01.726 --> 10:02.086 align:middle
Thank you.

10:02.246 --> 10:02.766 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

10:02.766 --> 10:03.386 align:middle
Sure, I'd be glad to.

10:03.906 --> 10:06.916 align:middle
So the virus outbreak
is something

10:07.366 --> 10:10.566 align:middle
that will require a
multifaceted response.

10:10.566 --> 10:12.796 align:middle
And that response will
come, in the first instance,

10:12.846 --> 10:16.096 align:middle
from health-care professionals
and health policy experts.

10:16.646 --> 10:19.806 align:middle
It will also come from fiscal
authorities should they

10:19.806 --> 10:21.616 align:middle
determine that a
response is appropriate.

10:22.596 --> 10:24.616 align:middle
And it would be-come
from many other public-

10:24.616 --> 10:27.146 align:middle
and private-sector actors:
businesses, schools,

10:27.236 --> 10:28.276 align:middle
state and local governments.

10:28.826 --> 10:30.926 align:middle
But there's also a role
for monetary policy.

10:31.376 --> 10:33.256 align:middle
Monetary policy can
be an effective tool

10:33.256 --> 10:35.086 align:middle
to support overall
economic activity.

10:35.416 --> 10:38.406 align:middle
We do recognize that a rate
cut will not reduce the rate

10:38.406 --> 10:38.976 align:middle
of infection.

10:38.976 --> 10:40.766 align:middle
It won't fix a broken
supply chain.

10:40.766 --> 10:41.326 align:middle
We get that.

10:41.506 --> 10:42.866 align:middle
We don't think we
have all the answers.

10:43.396 --> 10:46.006 align:middle
But we do believe that our
action will provide a meaningful

10:46.006 --> 10:46.996 align:middle
boost to the economy.

10:47.606 --> 10:50.356 align:middle
More specifically, it will
support accommodative financial

10:50.356 --> 10:51.656 align:middle
conditions and avoid
a tightening

10:51.656 --> 10:54.126 align:middle
of financial conditions
which can weigh on activity,

10:54.126 --> 10:57.056 align:middle
and it will help boost household
and business confidence.

10:57.326 --> 10:58.866 align:middle
That's why you're
seeing central banks

10:58.866 --> 11:01.136 align:middle
around the world responding
as they see appropriate

11:01.686 --> 11:03.796 align:middle
in their particular
institutional context.

11:05.396 --> 11:08.986 align:middle
YLAN MUI. Thanks.

11:08.986 --> 11:10.876 align:middle
Ylan Mui from CNBC.

11:10.876 --> 11:15.036 align:middle
Can you tell us what you
would do if the virus ends

11:15.036 --> 11:17.976 align:middle
up being contained
fairly quickly?

11:17.976 --> 11:21.296 align:middle
Would you envision the
Fed actually raising rates

11:21.296 --> 11:26.246 align:middle
in short order if the economic
damage doesn't occur the way

11:26.246 --> 11:27.956 align:middle
that you potentially fear?

11:27.956 --> 11:29.836 align:middle
And I have a second
question, which is,

11:30.056 --> 11:32.496 align:middle
President Trump was just
tweeting about you this morning

11:33.016 --> 11:36.216 align:middle
and talking about the
Fed's need to cut rates.

11:36.216 --> 11:38.586 align:middle
Did you feel any political
pressure to make this move?

11:38.986 --> 11:39.206 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

11:39.366 --> 11:42.006 align:middle
So, on your first
question, we're always going

11:42.006 --> 11:46.086 align:middle
to set monetary policy
at a given time in a way

11:46.086 --> 11:49.406 align:middle
that we think best serves
our dual-mandate goals,

11:49.576 --> 11:50.956 align:middle
and it's as simple as that.

11:51.416 --> 11:54.486 align:middle
So, you know, we'll-if
we get to a place

11:54.486 --> 11:56.436 align:middle
where we think it's
an appropriate time

11:56.436 --> 11:57.986 align:middle
to change the stance
of monetary policy,

11:57.986 --> 12:00.856 align:middle
we won't hesitate to do that.

12:00.856 --> 12:05.256 align:middle
I would also say that
it's-it's very important

12:05.256 --> 12:09.476 align:middle
that people understand that we
will always make our decisions

12:09.536 --> 12:11.216 align:middle
based on the best
thinking we have,

12:12.006 --> 12:17.116 align:middle
based on our-what we learned
from our outreach to businesses,

12:17.116 --> 12:19.696 align:middle
nonprofits, educational
institutions

12:19.696 --> 12:22.686 align:middle
that we get every cycle
through the Reserve Banks,

12:23.136 --> 12:25.076 align:middle
and the best analysis,
the best research.

12:25.076 --> 12:26.786 align:middle
We're always going to make
our decisions in the interests

12:26.786 --> 12:30.506 align:middle
of the American people
to carry forward and try

12:30.506 --> 12:32.766 align:middle
to achieve the mandates
that Congress has given us.

12:32.766 --> 12:33.436 align:middle
And we're never going

12:33.436 --> 12:36.556 align:middle
to consider any political
considerations whatsoever.

12:36.596 --> 12:38.996 align:middle
We will not do that,
and it's very important

12:38.996 --> 12:41.416 align:middle
that the public understand that.

12:41.856 --> 12:44.596 align:middle
CHRISTOPHER RUGABER.

12:45.426 --> 12:47.446 align:middle
Chris Rugaber, Associated Press.

12:47.446 --> 12:49.906 align:middle
I know-you mentioned a
little bit of outreach.

12:49.906 --> 12:52.336 align:middle
Some of the Fed Bank
presidents were talking

12:52.336 --> 12:53.836 align:middle
about last week how
much they were going

12:53.836 --> 12:55.516 align:middle
to listen to their contacts.

12:55.516 --> 12:57.916 align:middle
Have you gotten reports
sort of from the ground

12:57.916 --> 13:01.506 align:middle
from your business contacts,
nonprofits, and so forth

13:01.506 --> 13:03.926 align:middle
that have affected
your decisionmaking?

13:03.926 --> 13:05.836 align:middle
What are-what are you
hearing from all those folks?

13:05.836 --> 13:06.146 align:middle
Thank you.

13:07.226 --> 13:07.986 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

13:08.686 --> 13:13.476 align:middle
So we're-we are hearing-I
would just say, the effects are

13:13.476 --> 13:14.696 align:middle
at a very early stage.

13:14.896 --> 13:17.916 align:middle
But you are hearing concerns
from people-for example,

13:17.916 --> 13:18.986 align:middle
in the travel business

13:18.986 --> 13:22.136 align:middle
or the hotel business
and things like that.

13:22.136 --> 13:23.256 align:middle
That's-that's what
you are hearing.

13:23.306 --> 13:27.286 align:middle
But you don't see things
showing up in actual data.

13:27.286 --> 13:28.906 align:middle
You do see them showing
up in, sort of,

13:28.986 --> 13:31.406 align:middle
sentiment forecast indicators
and things like that.

13:32.016 --> 13:33.286 align:middle
We expect that will continue.

13:33.286 --> 13:34.946 align:middle
It will probably grow.

13:35.466 --> 13:38.256 align:middle
And that's one of the reasons
why we've come to the view

13:38.256 --> 13:40.646 align:middle
that it would be appropriate
for us today to move

13:41.306 --> 13:46.646 align:middle
to support the economy.

13:47.586 --> 14:05.466 align:middle
And that's what we've done.

14:18.796 --> 14:22.986 align:middle
Thanks very much.

